00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.371
we have some partners
and other vendors

00:01:49.371 --> 00:01:51.120
that will come up on
stage and demonstrate

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:55.310
how their tools can help
make your apps more secure.

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:57.850
As a bit of a high level
overview, a couple of things.

00:01:57.850 --> 00:01:59.470
One, we have the camera emoji.

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:01.511
That means pull out your
phone and take a picture

00:02:01.511 --> 00:02:03.170
or otherwise make a
mental note of it.

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:05.720
But the first important link
that we're going to be sharing

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:08.250
is Node Sec Roadmap.

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:12.080
So if you go to this link it
has a ton of different threats.

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:13.910
A lot of them are kind
of specific or kind

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:17.060
of in the context of Node JS,
but the threats themselves

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:18.650
are general and can
apply to whatever

00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:20.750
language or environment
you're running in.

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:22.139
So we're going to be using Node.

00:02:22.139 --> 00:02:24.680
We obviously have some pretty
deep technical expertise there,

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:28.301
but it should apply to whatever
language you're working in.

00:02:28.301 --> 00:02:31.040
MYLES BORINS: So we just use
the term threat environment.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:34.090
And you're probably wondering,
what is a threat environment?

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:37.010
Here's the definition from a
software engineer Mike Samuel.

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:39.850
He's actually the engineer
who put together the Node Sec

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:41.440
Roadmap that we just showed.

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:43.990
A threat environment is a
set of targets under attack

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:47.230
and the tools that might
be used to get at them.

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:49.450
It's important to know
that different targets are

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:52.420
vulnerable to different
tools in different ways.

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:55.240
So an application running
on one type of provider

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:58.090
is going to be vulnerable
to different types of tools

00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:00.220
than an application running
in another environment.

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:02.830
If you're running an
application in a virtual machine

00:03:02.830 --> 00:03:05.820
that you are maintaining
yourself on your own silicon,

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:07.790
there is a different
threat environment

00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:10.390
than if you're running
something on App Engine.

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:12.010
And knowing that
threat environment

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:13.630
helps you know
what tools you need

00:03:13.630 --> 00:03:15.370
to have to protect
yourself from attack.

00:03:18.444 --> 00:03:20.860
So we're going to talk about
a couple of different threats

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:21.569
today.

00:03:21.569 --> 00:03:23.110
Quickly listing them
off, we're going

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:26.380
to talk about zero days;
malicious and low quality code;

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:29.200
remote code execution;
denial of service,

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:31.090
classically referred
to as a DOS;

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:32.980
we'll talk about
leaked credentials;

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:36.370
we'll talk about data
exfiltration; query injection;

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:39.580
and one of my favorites, which
doesn't always make these lists

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:41.050
for developer experience.

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:43.372
We'll dig into that in a bit.

00:03:43.372 --> 00:03:44.330
MICHAEL MCDONALD: Cool.

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:47.960
So we decided the best way of
showing common vulnerabilities

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:51.620
is to build a really terrible
app and show it to you.

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:55.160
So Myles went on vacation
over this past weekend

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:58.490
and I was hounding him
ceaselessly on Friday afternoon

00:03:58.490 --> 00:04:00.950
to build an application for
our demo, and he of course

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:02.280
just wants to go on vacation.

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:04.850
So he actually builds the app
on the way back from Tahoe.

00:04:04.850 --> 00:04:06.495
So it's like a three,
four hour drive,

00:04:06.495 --> 00:04:08.120
he hacked together
a bunch of functions

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:10.460
to build a content
management solution.

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:13.190
And obviously, written any code
written in the back of a car

00:04:13.190 --> 00:04:15.440
is bound to be
absolutely horrific.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:18.690
So he has saved
mark down function.

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:20.634
By the way, if no one
is familiar with it,

00:04:20.634 --> 00:04:21.800
we're using Cloud Functions.

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:24.620
So Cloud Functions is Google
Cloud's platforms functions

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:26.390
as a service offering,
which is kind

00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:29.550
of the prototypical
Serverless offering.

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:31.910
And so he wrote a function
that takes in mark down,

00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:35.210
converts it to HTML and
writes it to Cloud SQL.

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:36.620
And then we have
another function

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:39.797
that conserve that
HTML from Cloud SQL.

00:04:39.797 --> 00:04:42.380
We're actually even just going
to focus on that first function

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:44.754
because trust me there are a
lot of things wrong with it.

00:04:47.052 --> 00:04:48.260
MYLES BORINS: So this is fun.

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:50.990
You might think that we're
just making a fun joke,

00:04:50.990 --> 00:04:55.430
but I legitimately wrote this
in the car on the way home

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:56.092
from Tahoe.

00:04:56.092 --> 00:04:57.800
And before we dive
into the code I'm just

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:00.160
going to show you that this
is live and it actually works.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:01.785
This is the payload
that we're sending.

00:05:01.785 --> 00:05:06.340
It's right in the body like any
good post request should do.

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:08.280
The author is my name.

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:10.340
The title, Oh my God, the
haxors are at it again.

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:13.196
And the markdown,
drop tables lol.

00:05:13.196 --> 00:05:15.710
We're going to go and
post that and we're

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:17.720
going to have a response
from the database.

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:19.560
Because your raw
database response

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:22.400
is what you should
always be returning.

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:24.480
And we could see
that this works.

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:27.170
So let's take a look at
the source really quickly.

00:05:27.170 --> 00:05:29.540
You could see that we're
promisifying stuff.

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:31.310
Because we're using
Node 8, which we just

00:05:31.310 --> 00:05:34.220
announced yesterday, which means
we can use async await, which

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:36.020
I absolutely adore.

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:38.082
We have all of our
credentials in line

00:05:38.082 --> 00:05:40.040
so we never have to look
at that piece of paper

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:41.360
that I have on my desk.

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:43.940
We immediately create a
connection to the pool.

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:46.910
We're doing this with
creating a pool for the MySQL

00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:50.270
direct connection so that it
will be able to pool resources

00:05:50.270 --> 00:05:52.040
when we have multiple
functions go up

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:56.004
and be able to reconnect if
we have any connection issues.

00:05:56.004 --> 00:05:58.170
This is also another feature
that we just announced.

00:05:58.170 --> 00:06:02.150
You can do direct connect
from Cloud Functions now.

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:05.960
In the make post async function
here, we're going to go,

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:07.970
we're going to convert
the markdown that's

00:06:07.970 --> 00:06:09.837
been sent to us into HTML.

00:06:09.837 --> 00:06:11.420
We're going to make
a new data object.

00:06:11.420 --> 00:06:13.520
We're going to post directly in.

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:16.430
You can see all the information
that we're doing in line here.

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:19.012
Nice and sanitized, or not.

00:06:19.012 --> 00:06:20.720
Then we're going to
promise returning it.

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:22.303
And we just do a
little try catch here

00:06:22.303 --> 00:06:24.860
where we await the
results of make post.

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:30.590
And then we respond with
the results themselves.

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:31.670
Back to the slides.

00:06:31.670 --> 00:06:32.240
MICHAEL MCDONALD:
There are definitely

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:33.140
some issues with that.

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:33.860
MYLES BORINS: Yeah.

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:35.210
MICHAEL MCDONALD:
So the first thing

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:37.418
that we're going to talk
about, if we were completely

00:06:37.418 --> 00:06:38.793
unaware of these
vulnerabilities,

00:06:38.793 --> 00:06:40.793
we might run into something
called the zero day.

00:06:40.793 --> 00:06:42.855
So how many people are
familiar with zero days?

00:06:42.855 --> 00:06:44.080
Couple people?

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:45.860
OK, half the room.

00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:48.740
So normally when
you have a threat,

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:51.060
you have this thing called
the window of vulnerability.

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:53.810
So from the time that the
vulnerability is disclosed,

00:06:53.810 --> 00:06:57.110
ideally someone sends the email
to security at your company

00:06:57.110 --> 00:06:59.932
.com and says, hey, I
think there's an issue.

00:06:59.932 --> 00:07:02.390
Hopefully they're not selling
it on the internet to someone

00:07:02.390 --> 00:07:04.056
else who's going to
go and exploit that,

00:07:04.056 --> 00:07:07.430
but ideally in the best
case you the vendor

00:07:07.430 --> 00:07:08.690
get that information.

00:07:08.690 --> 00:07:10.610
You tell your engineers,
or you go and patch

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:12.800
that vulnerability yourself.

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:15.080
And ideally, you can actually
patch the vulnerability

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:18.050
and get users to apply it
before any exploit is actually

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:19.760
found or used.

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:22.767
But if the exploit is active
before things are fixed,

00:07:22.767 --> 00:07:24.350
you have this window
of vulnerability,

00:07:24.350 --> 00:07:26.540
your kind of danger
zone of when users

00:07:26.540 --> 00:07:29.090
are likely to encounter it.

00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:31.220
In a zero day, that's
entirely flipped.

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:32.720
The whole premise
of a zero day is

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:35.270
the exploit is actually
active and out in the wild

00:07:35.270 --> 00:07:36.800
before anyone knows about it.

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:39.530
Or you become immediately
aware on day zero

00:07:39.530 --> 00:07:41.090
that there is a problem.

00:07:41.090 --> 00:07:44.400
And then you have to scramble
to go and fix all of your code

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:46.740
and hopefully get
users to update it.

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:49.820
So how do you stay ahead of
what zero days potentially

00:07:49.820 --> 00:07:52.690
exist to avoid those?

00:07:52.690 --> 00:07:54.094
MYLES BORINS: So acronyms.

00:07:54.094 --> 00:07:55.510
We're at a Google
conference so we

00:07:55.510 --> 00:07:56.965
have lots of acronyms for you.

00:07:56.965 --> 00:07:59.620
CVE, CNA CWE.

00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:02.800
So the first one, CVE,
the common vulnerability

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:04.210
and exposures.

00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:08.800
This is a numerated list
that you can get from MITRE.

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:11.590
Every single CVE is a number
that you can go and look up.

00:08:11.590 --> 00:08:14.470
It will give you the name
of the dependency or project

00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:17.440
that has a vulnerability and
what that vulnerability is.

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:21.220
With a CVE number we're able
to know what's going on.

00:08:21.220 --> 00:08:22.930
If you're running
a certain project,

00:08:22.930 --> 00:08:25.130
like Node in
production for example,

00:08:25.130 --> 00:08:27.640
you can cross-reference
against known CVEs,

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:30.340
and know if the version that
you're using is vulnerable

00:08:30.340 --> 00:08:32.950
or not, and what vulnerabilities
you're vulnerable to.

00:08:32.950 --> 00:08:36.130
You have a CNA, which is
a CVE numbering authority.

00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:39.880
This is who assigns a CVE
number to a vulnerability.

00:08:39.880 --> 00:08:41.480
In the Node project,
for example,

00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:44.980
we actually get pre-assigned
blocks of CVEs from MITRE

00:08:44.980 --> 00:08:48.910
directly and act as our own
CNA associating CVE numbers

00:08:48.910 --> 00:08:50.680
with vulnerabilities
as we discover them.

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:53.800
Before we became a CNA, we were
actually using MITRE directly

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:54.760
as a CNA.

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:56.140
So every time a
new vulnerability

00:08:56.140 --> 00:08:58.150
was about to be
disclosed, we would

00:08:58.150 --> 00:08:59.830
write MITRE with
that information

00:08:59.830 --> 00:09:02.140
and then they would get
back to us with a number.

00:09:02.140 --> 00:09:03.850
This actually resulted
in times where

00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:07.565
we would have to put the patch
out before we had the number

00:09:07.565 --> 00:09:09.190
and update with the
number later, which

00:09:09.190 --> 00:09:12.506
was why we went through the
process of becoming a CNA.

00:09:12.506 --> 00:09:13.880
The third one that
we have there,

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:17.440
CWE, a common
weakness enumeration.

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:19.030
CWE are a list of
numbers that you

00:09:19.030 --> 00:09:21.130
can use to reference
certain types

00:09:21.130 --> 00:09:24.290
of vulnerabilities and known
patterns that are problematic.

00:09:24.290 --> 00:09:26.260
So if you are a
security researcher,

00:09:26.260 --> 00:09:28.930
you may use a CWE
when reporting.

00:09:28.930 --> 00:09:31.960
So for example, using
all these in a sentence,

00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:34.180
an open source project
can use the MITRE CNA

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:36.920
to issue a CVE for a
patched vulnerability.

00:09:36.920 --> 00:09:39.340
When a security researcher
reports a vulnerability,

00:09:39.340 --> 00:09:42.377
they may include a relevant CWE.

00:09:42.377 --> 00:09:43.460
MICHAEL MCDONALD: Awesome.

00:09:43.460 --> 00:09:44.825
Thanks, Myles.

00:09:44.825 --> 00:09:46.200
MYLES BORINS:
These links up here

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:50.460
go to CVE and CWE, which
are both on mitre.org.

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:53.722
So you can go through and look
at what CVEs are out there,

00:09:53.722 --> 00:09:54.930
what you have to worry about.

00:09:54.930 --> 00:09:57.210
And if you're thinking
about reporting things,

00:09:57.210 --> 00:10:01.320
you can go to the CWE to
include a CWE with your report

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:06.410
to a project when you're
reporting a vulnerability.

00:10:06.410 --> 00:10:09.299
So vulnerabilities go all
the way down the stack.

00:10:09.299 --> 00:10:10.840
When we start off
and think about it,

00:10:10.840 --> 00:10:12.760
you have your hardware.

00:10:12.760 --> 00:10:15.580
I'm not going to talk about some
of the hardware vulnerabilities

00:10:15.580 --> 00:10:16.690
that happened
recently because they

00:10:16.690 --> 00:10:18.310
caused me existential
dread, so we're

00:10:18.310 --> 00:10:20.230
going to move up the stack.

00:10:20.230 --> 00:10:22.190
And that moves to
the operating system.

00:10:22.190 --> 00:10:26.110
These can be vulnerabilities
in things like Linux,

00:10:26.110 --> 00:10:28.250
it could be in POSIX itself.

00:10:28.250 --> 00:10:30.310
We had a really fun
one back in the day

00:10:30.310 --> 00:10:32.350
with Real Path 3 in Node.

00:10:32.350 --> 00:10:34.400
Find me later, I'll
tell you about that one.

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:37.330
We move up the stack and we
move to our language runtime.

00:10:37.330 --> 00:10:38.530
This is Node itself.

00:10:38.530 --> 00:10:40.730
There may be
vulnerabilities in Node,

00:10:40.730 --> 00:10:45.400
such as regular expression DoSes
which we've had in the past

00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:47.200
and patched fairly recently.

00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:48.994
You move up the
stack and you're now

00:10:48.994 --> 00:10:50.410
into your dependencies,
everything

00:10:50.410 --> 00:10:52.644
that you've installed via NPM.

00:10:52.644 --> 00:10:54.060
We've seen some
recently and we'll

00:10:54.060 --> 00:10:56.710
dig in to vulnerabilities
that could affect you

00:10:56.710 --> 00:10:57.704
via your dependencies.

00:10:57.704 --> 00:10:59.620
And finally you reached
the top of your stack,

00:10:59.620 --> 00:11:01.540
you reach your application,
and it's possible

00:11:01.540 --> 00:11:03.690
that you're writing your
own vulnerabilities.

00:11:03.690 --> 00:11:05.440
The thing it's important
to see about this

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:07.060
all the way down
is like, while you

00:11:07.060 --> 00:11:10.060
need to be managing your
own application code

00:11:10.060 --> 00:11:12.550
and making sure that your
team is writing secure code,

00:11:12.550 --> 00:11:14.950
there is a lot of
stuff underneath you

00:11:14.950 --> 00:11:16.930
that can be causing
you to be vulnerable

00:11:16.930 --> 00:11:17.980
in a transparent way.

00:11:17.980 --> 00:11:19.640
You may have no idea.

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:22.450
Now, a great thing
about running Serverless

00:11:22.450 --> 00:11:26.665
is that everything below your
dependencies is managed by us.

00:11:26.665 --> 00:11:28.540
So if you deploy something
to Cloud Functions

00:11:28.540 --> 00:11:31.430
or you deploy something
to App Engine standard,

00:11:31.430 --> 00:11:34.540
whenever we get
notified of new CVEs

00:11:34.540 --> 00:11:36.200
whenever new patches
are available,

00:11:36.200 --> 00:11:39.950
we're able to patch the language
runtime, the operating system,

00:11:39.950 --> 00:11:42.070
and even replace the
hardware from under you

00:11:42.070 --> 00:11:44.650
without any downtime
or you having

00:11:44.650 --> 00:11:46.660
to make any changes on your end.

00:11:46.660 --> 00:11:49.540
When the Node project does
a new security release,

00:11:49.540 --> 00:11:52.139
we are, in a timely
manner, updating

00:11:52.139 --> 00:11:53.680
all of the functions
that are already

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:56.230
deployed so the next time
that it runs it's not

00:11:56.230 --> 00:11:58.550
going to be vulnerable.

00:11:58.550 --> 00:12:03.530
This is one of the advantages
to using Serverless.

00:12:03.530 --> 00:12:05.930
So one of the things
that we talked about

00:12:05.930 --> 00:12:08.200
are the dependencies,
which is bringing

00:12:08.200 --> 00:12:12.200
in malicious or low quality
code into your project.

00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:15.364
So there's lots of ways
that this can get in.

00:12:15.364 --> 00:12:16.530
You can have malicious code.

00:12:16.530 --> 00:12:19.010
This is code that
is explicitly trying

00:12:19.010 --> 00:12:21.320
to be malicious and do
things like pulling things

00:12:21.320 --> 00:12:22.400
from pastebin.

00:12:22.400 --> 00:12:24.129
You can have low
quality dependencies.

00:12:24.129 --> 00:12:26.420
These are just packages that
weren't very well designed

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:31.070
and introduce unexpected
bugs and performance

00:12:31.070 --> 00:12:32.810
problems into your code.

00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:36.170
Regular expression DoSes are
some of the most active ones

00:12:36.170 --> 00:12:37.340
that you'll find here.

00:12:37.340 --> 00:12:38.750
And also unintended requires.

00:12:38.750 --> 00:12:42.320
It's possible that you
unintentionally require things

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:47.880
into your project because
of dynamic dependencies.

00:12:47.880 --> 00:12:51.540
So one of the things that
bad dependencies may do

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:55.830
or even may happen in your own
code is remote code execution.

00:12:55.830 --> 00:12:57.360
How many people in
the audience are

00:12:57.360 --> 00:13:00.040
shipping eval in production?

00:13:00.040 --> 00:13:02.550
MICHAEL MCDONALD: Or the
equivalent in your language.

00:13:02.550 --> 00:13:03.600
MYLES BORINS: And
you may actually

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:05.391
be shipping eval and
not even realizing it.

00:13:05.391 --> 00:13:08.640
If you're using VM run in
this context or any of the VM

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:10.890
objects in Node, that
actually is still

00:13:10.890 --> 00:13:12.450
sharing the same context.

00:13:12.450 --> 00:13:14.850
And you may be leaking
and changing your context

00:13:14.850 --> 00:13:17.970
if you're running
unsanitized or unknown code.

00:13:17.970 --> 00:13:21.215
So remote code execution,
an example of this

00:13:21.215 --> 00:13:22.590
actually happened
fairly recently

00:13:22.590 --> 00:13:24.510
with the ESLint
security incident.

00:13:24.510 --> 00:13:26.850
Is anyone familiar with it?

00:13:26.850 --> 00:13:31.260
So last week,
ESLint scope module

00:13:31.260 --> 00:13:34.050
was hijacked to steal
NPMRC credentials.

00:13:34.050 --> 00:13:36.930
One of the maintainers
of the ESLint project

00:13:36.930 --> 00:13:39.367
had their machine compromised.

00:13:39.367 --> 00:13:40.950
The person who
compromised the machine

00:13:40.950 --> 00:13:43.560
then published a new version
of the ESLint scope module

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:44.970
as a patch update.

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:47.130
The ESLint scope module
was dependent by ESLint.

00:13:47.130 --> 00:13:49.030
And if any of you
write JavaScript,

00:13:49.030 --> 00:13:51.620
ESLint is the most
widely used tool

00:13:51.620 --> 00:13:53.370
for linting code, which
is the way that we

00:13:53.370 --> 00:13:55.950
know that we write errors
because dynamic languages are

00:13:55.950 --> 00:13:56.800
fun.

00:13:56.800 --> 00:14:00.240
So when this
vulnerability happened,

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:03.210
the credentials were stolen,
malicious code was published.

00:14:03.210 --> 00:14:06.120
That malicious code was
pulling down a pastebin link

00:14:06.120 --> 00:14:08.040
and then executing it via eval.

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:10.680
And at that point, it was
then uploading credentials

00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:12.690
or attempting to
upload credentials

00:14:12.690 --> 00:14:13.980
to a centralized service.

00:14:13.980 --> 00:14:15.960
This is what's
formally known as worm.

00:14:15.960 --> 00:14:17.790
It was compromised
and then trying

00:14:17.790 --> 00:14:20.484
to compromise other packages
and spread across the ecosystem.

00:14:20.484 --> 00:14:21.900
There's a couple
of different ways

00:14:21.900 --> 00:14:23.010
that we could
defend against this.

00:14:23.010 --> 00:14:24.870
If you were using
explicit lock files,

00:14:24.870 --> 00:14:27.150
you wouldn't have updated
to the patch version

00:14:27.150 --> 00:14:28.831
if you were sticking
to the lock file.

00:14:28.831 --> 00:14:31.080
And if you had had two factor
authentication turned on

00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:33.210
on your account, the worm
will not be able to spread.

00:14:33.210 --> 00:14:34.620
Because when it
tried to publish,

00:14:34.620 --> 00:14:36.460
it would be asking
for your credentials.

00:14:36.460 --> 00:14:39.030
I think this is a really
great example of where

00:14:39.030 --> 00:14:43.290
the inconvenience of 2FA
pays off in the not having

00:14:43.290 --> 00:14:46.640
all of your modules stolen.

00:14:46.640 --> 00:14:49.310
So this was how the
attacker did it.

00:14:49.310 --> 00:14:52.760
They modified the package JSON
in both ESLint scope and ESLint

00:14:52.760 --> 00:14:55.490
config, adding a
post install script

00:14:55.490 --> 00:14:57.760
that ran this lib build.js.

00:14:57.760 --> 00:15:02.660
And lib bill.js called
an hdbs get on pastebin,

00:15:02.660 --> 00:15:04.530
got the information,
and avowed it.

00:15:04.530 --> 00:15:07.160
This right here, that line
is why the worm did not

00:15:07.160 --> 00:15:09.620
spread as wide as it could.

00:15:09.620 --> 00:15:12.430
Generally in Node when
you're doing something.on,

00:15:12.430 --> 00:15:15.410
you want to be concatenating
the entire string

00:15:15.410 --> 00:15:18.150
and then do on end
to evaluate it.

00:15:18.150 --> 00:15:19.580
What we can tell
from this attack,

00:15:19.580 --> 00:15:21.996
though, is that we had a really
sophisticated attacker who

00:15:21.996 --> 00:15:25.880
just was not well-versed
in the Node APIs.

00:15:25.880 --> 00:15:29.570
So thankfully this caused
a whole bunch of crashes

00:15:29.570 --> 00:15:30.200
to happen.

00:15:30.200 --> 00:15:32.600
And then those
crashes, when it tried

00:15:32.600 --> 00:15:35.900
to avow CNC wasn't a full string
it was how people noticed it

00:15:35.900 --> 00:15:37.340
within 10 minutes.

00:15:37.340 --> 00:15:40.640
And this is, I think, one
of the really great examples

00:15:40.640 --> 00:15:43.086
of how on top of it
the Node community is.

00:15:43.086 --> 00:15:44.960
Because the second that
people knew about it,

00:15:44.960 --> 00:15:46.280
everyone knew about it.

00:15:46.280 --> 00:15:50.000
And things were
getting locked down.

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:52.190
And to come talk
a little bit more

00:15:52.190 --> 00:15:54.080
about this and some
ways of protecting

00:15:54.080 --> 00:15:57.050
your projects we have CJ
Silverio, the CTO of NPM.

00:15:57.050 --> 00:15:58.534
Quick round of applause.

00:15:58.534 --> 00:16:03.174
[APPLAUSE]

00:16:03.174 --> 00:16:04.060
Hi, CJ.

00:16:04.060 --> 00:16:05.210
How are you doing today?

00:16:05.210 --> 00:16:06.290
CJ SILVERIO: Pretty good.

00:16:06.290 --> 00:16:08.831
You told me it was a story about
a week that I had was not so

00:16:08.831 --> 00:16:10.146
good, but it's good now.

00:16:10.146 --> 00:16:12.270
MYLES BORINS: Thankfully
we talked about it before.

00:16:12.270 --> 00:16:13.730
CJ SILVERIO: Yeah.

00:16:13.730 --> 00:16:16.944
MYLES BORINS: So CJ's going
to come up and demo something.

00:16:16.944 --> 00:16:19.360
CJ's in our insecure project
that we were just showing you

00:16:19.360 --> 00:16:22.026
where I brought in a whole bunch
of dependencies really quickly.

00:16:22.026 --> 00:16:24.190
And CJ, what are you
going to do for us today?

00:16:24.190 --> 00:16:25.606
CJ SILVERIO: I'm
going to show you

00:16:25.606 --> 00:16:29.470
how we help you close that gap
between when a vulnerability is

00:16:29.470 --> 00:16:32.710
found, it's been patched,
but you're still not

00:16:32.710 --> 00:16:35.950
in the green zone because
you don't know about it

00:16:35.950 --> 00:16:39.140
and you haven't applied
that patch to your project.

00:16:39.140 --> 00:16:42.670
So I'm going to demonstrate a
really pretty nifty new thing

00:16:42.670 --> 00:16:45.430
that we've done at NPM
to allow you to find out

00:16:45.430 --> 00:16:48.160
how to do something about
this without interrupting

00:16:48.160 --> 00:16:49.790
your normal workflow.

00:16:49.790 --> 00:16:52.160
Here I'm in Myles'
insecure project,

00:16:52.160 --> 00:16:57.010
and I'm just going to run
NPM install like I always do.

00:16:57.010 --> 00:16:58.660
Maybe I'm installing
a new dependency,

00:16:58.660 --> 00:17:01.570
maybe I'm just running
install to do development.

00:17:01.570 --> 00:17:05.619
Oh, holy high severity, Batman.

00:17:05.619 --> 00:17:07.240
I have a high
security vulnerability

00:17:07.240 --> 00:17:08.720
that I didn't know about.

00:17:08.720 --> 00:17:11.619
So now I can find out more
information about that

00:17:11.619 --> 00:17:12.650
if I want.

00:17:12.650 --> 00:17:15.020
Notice I didn't do anything
special to find out.

00:17:15.020 --> 00:17:16.960
Now I'm going to
run a new command

00:17:16.960 --> 00:17:19.440
for NPM called NPM audit.

00:17:19.440 --> 00:17:22.670
Here I find that
marked, the library

00:17:22.670 --> 00:17:26.170
I've been using to render
my markdown for my app,

00:17:26.170 --> 00:17:29.810
has a regular expression
denial of service.

00:17:29.810 --> 00:17:31.270
OK, what does that mean?

00:17:31.270 --> 00:17:32.680
I'm probably vulnerable to it.

00:17:32.680 --> 00:17:34.430
What should I do to fix it?

00:17:34.430 --> 00:17:35.890
Well that's easy, too.

00:17:35.890 --> 00:17:37.870
NPM audit fix.

00:17:37.870 --> 00:17:41.770
The great thing here
is if we can give you

00:17:41.770 --> 00:17:44.590
a patched version of that
dependency without violating

00:17:44.590 --> 00:17:47.770
your Semver requirements that
are in your package JSON,

00:17:47.770 --> 00:17:48.869
we will do it.

00:17:48.869 --> 00:17:51.160
And we will give you the fix
right here without out you

00:17:51.160 --> 00:17:55.570
having to do anything
more than just that.

00:17:55.570 --> 00:17:56.950
Fixed one vulnerability.

00:17:56.950 --> 00:18:01.720
Now if I run NPM install, clean.

00:18:01.720 --> 00:18:03.730
I'm in that green zone now.

00:18:03.730 --> 00:18:05.480
I'm safe, I've patched.

00:18:05.480 --> 00:18:06.980
MYLES BORINS: That's
really awesome.

00:18:06.980 --> 00:18:08.930
And-- [APPLAUSE]

00:18:08.930 --> 00:18:09.680
CJ SILVERIO: Yeah.

00:18:12.452 --> 00:18:14.410
MYLES BORINS: Can you
tell me a little bit more

00:18:14.410 --> 00:18:17.410
about how it knew that
there was a vulnerability?

00:18:17.410 --> 00:18:20.140
CJ SILVERIO: We have taken
the Node security project

00:18:20.140 --> 00:18:21.850
vulnerability
database-- which is

00:18:21.850 --> 00:18:25.570
the collective work of the Node
ecosystem, which takes this

00:18:25.570 --> 00:18:27.730
very seriously-- and
we've integrated that

00:18:27.730 --> 00:18:29.060
in with NPM install.

00:18:29.060 --> 00:18:32.050
So every time you do
a read of packages,

00:18:32.050 --> 00:18:36.540
we have that security
data passed down.

00:18:36.540 --> 00:18:38.560
Even if you're pulling
for your local cache

00:18:38.560 --> 00:18:41.980
we can tell you that
we found something

00:18:41.980 --> 00:18:44.411
that you didn't know about
last time you ran an install.

00:18:44.411 --> 00:18:45.910
MYLES BORINS: That's
really amazing.

00:18:45.910 --> 00:18:48.460
And then we were just talking
before about two factor

00:18:48.460 --> 00:18:49.349
authentication.

00:18:49.349 --> 00:18:51.182
I believe NPM has some
new stuff coming down

00:18:51.182 --> 00:18:51.640
the pipeline for it?

00:18:51.640 --> 00:18:52.870
CJ SILVERIO: Yes, we do.

00:18:52.870 --> 00:18:56.110
The ESLint maintainers, who
also had a very bad week,

00:18:56.110 --> 00:18:59.631
could have done a
couple of things

00:18:59.631 --> 00:19:00.880
to protect themselves from it.

00:19:00.880 --> 00:19:04.840
They could have turned on two
factor auth for all rights

00:19:04.840 --> 00:19:08.155
so that any time
they typed publish

00:19:08.155 --> 00:19:09.940
for any package they
own, they would have

00:19:09.940 --> 00:19:11.710
to provide a one time password.

00:19:11.710 --> 00:19:15.040
We've just rolled out
last week in response

00:19:15.040 --> 00:19:18.100
to this a brand new feature
where you can protect

00:19:18.100 --> 00:19:22.900
a package with two factor auth
so that any time any maintainer

00:19:22.900 --> 00:19:25.330
publishes it, whether
they've turned on 2FA or not,

00:19:25.330 --> 00:19:29.770
they have to enable it,
provide a one time password,

00:19:29.770 --> 00:19:31.120
and install.

00:19:31.120 --> 00:19:32.440
It is an inconvenience.

00:19:32.440 --> 00:19:36.550
But if you published NPM,
please protect your packages.

00:19:36.550 --> 00:19:41.910
If you are using a very highly
popular package, enable this.

00:19:41.910 --> 00:19:43.630
And this is in the
NPMC [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:43.630 --> 00:19:45.700
right now website,
you just flip it on.

00:19:45.700 --> 00:19:46.700
MYLES BORINS: Wonderful.

00:19:46.700 --> 00:19:47.620
Thank you so much, CJ.

00:19:47.620 --> 00:19:48.786
CJ SILVERIO: You're welcome.

00:19:48.786 --> 00:19:49.348
Thank you.

00:19:49.348 --> 00:19:52.216
[APPLAUSE]

00:19:56.530 --> 00:19:58.650
MICHAEL MCDONALD: Cool,
so we saw right there

00:19:58.650 --> 00:20:00.192
regular expression
denial of service.

00:20:00.192 --> 00:20:02.650
How many people feel like they
know what denial of services

00:20:02.650 --> 00:20:03.372
are?

00:20:03.372 --> 00:20:04.080
A couple of them?

00:20:04.080 --> 00:20:04.930
MYLES BORINS: Mike.

00:20:04.930 --> 00:20:05.430
Mike.

00:20:05.430 --> 00:20:07.090
MICHAEL MCDONALD:
Oh God, stop, stop.

00:20:07.090 --> 00:20:08.214
I don't have time for this.

00:20:08.214 --> 00:20:09.269
MYLES BORINS: OK.

00:20:09.269 --> 00:20:10.560
MICHAEL MCDONALD: There you go.

00:20:10.560 --> 00:20:13.684
So Myles just exhibited
a very classic example

00:20:13.684 --> 00:20:14.850
of denial of service, right?

00:20:14.850 --> 00:20:17.940
You have traffic coming
into your application,

00:20:17.940 --> 00:20:21.750
and malicious traffic
then starts bothering you.

00:20:21.750 --> 00:20:24.360
Maybe it's taking up
network resources,

00:20:24.360 --> 00:20:28.170
or your CPU just hits 100%,
you can't do anything anymore.

00:20:28.170 --> 00:20:30.000
You can't focus on
that task that you

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:32.650
were supposed to do earlier.

00:20:32.650 --> 00:20:35.050
But there are also a
number of other kinds

00:20:35.050 --> 00:20:36.430
of denials of service.

00:20:36.430 --> 00:20:38.140
So the resource
exhaustion case that we

00:20:38.140 --> 00:20:40.420
showed there of, say,
running out of network

00:20:40.420 --> 00:20:42.169
or running out of CPU.

00:20:42.169 --> 00:20:44.710
But you also have a couple of
other categories of things that

00:20:44.710 --> 00:20:46.362
could cause denials of service.

00:20:46.362 --> 00:20:48.070
The regular expression
DoS, for instance,

00:20:48.070 --> 00:20:50.290
could be kind of
a code error issue

00:20:50.290 --> 00:20:52.600
where maybe a well-formed
request comes in,

00:20:52.600 --> 00:20:56.140
but it triggers some bug
in the code that ends up

00:20:56.140 --> 00:20:58.300
creating a resource
exhaustion or maybe there's

00:20:58.300 --> 00:21:00.700
other expensive computation.

00:21:00.700 --> 00:21:02.320
So for instance, we
were doing crypto

00:21:02.320 --> 00:21:06.100
on the main thread in Node
and basically DoSed ourselves.

00:21:06.100 --> 00:21:08.740
And then my personal
favorite denial of service

00:21:08.740 --> 00:21:10.720
is an economic
denial of service.

00:21:10.720 --> 00:21:13.300
Where someone-- one of the
great things about Serverless

00:21:13.300 --> 00:21:14.650
is that it scales infinitely.

00:21:14.650 --> 00:21:16.360
You keep throwing
traffic at it and it

00:21:16.360 --> 00:21:18.250
keeps scaling, which
potentially means

00:21:18.250 --> 00:21:19.960
that it keeps billing you.

00:21:19.960 --> 00:21:22.870
So if you have a budget set,
or there are quotas in place,

00:21:22.870 --> 00:21:26.310
those could be throttling you.

00:21:26.310 --> 00:21:30.510
So we showed the kind of classic
denial of service of a resource

00:21:30.510 --> 00:21:31.220
exhaustion.

00:21:31.220 --> 00:21:34.680
We'll now show a really fun
one, the hug of death of DoSing

00:21:34.680 --> 00:21:35.760
yourself.

00:21:35.760 --> 00:21:38.160
So you may have external
traffic coming in.

00:21:38.160 --> 00:21:40.560
And as your Cloud
Functions are scaling up,

00:21:40.560 --> 00:21:42.990
maybe they're writing
to that SQL database,

00:21:42.990 --> 00:21:44.580
you run out of file descriptors.

00:21:44.580 --> 00:21:47.340
Or there are some
other finite resources,

00:21:47.340 --> 00:21:50.850
and you overload your
database, and it falls over.

00:21:50.850 --> 00:21:53.430
So we have a really
cool new tool.

00:21:53.430 --> 00:21:54.610
So scaling controls.

00:21:54.610 --> 00:21:58.370
So again, it's really great to
have the infinite scalability

00:21:58.370 --> 00:21:59.700
of functions as a service.

00:21:59.700 --> 00:22:01.980
But sometimes,
because not everything

00:22:01.980 --> 00:22:04.380
is serverless and
amazing under the hood,

00:22:04.380 --> 00:22:07.260
you want to, say, limit to
only 100 functions can run.

00:22:07.260 --> 00:22:10.250
Because you may only have 100
file descriptors or whatever.

00:22:10.250 --> 00:22:12.450
There is some limiting
resource in the backend.

00:22:12.450 --> 00:22:15.300
You can deploy your functions
with the max instances flag

00:22:15.300 --> 00:22:16.330
and limit it.

00:22:16.330 --> 00:22:18.960
So once you've done
that, your functions

00:22:18.960 --> 00:22:21.210
will start scaling
up, and they'll

00:22:21.210 --> 00:22:24.180
hit that max instances
limit and peter off.

00:22:24.180 --> 00:22:26.800
So traffic, you'll start
providing back pressure

00:22:26.800 --> 00:22:28.164
up to your clients.

00:22:28.164 --> 00:22:30.330
Maybe the clients will force
retries or do something

00:22:30.330 --> 00:22:31.300
like that.

00:22:31.300 --> 00:22:34.020
But your database will be fine.

00:22:34.020 --> 00:22:34.680
A quick note.

00:22:34.680 --> 00:22:36.870
So we have a couple of
different Serverless products

00:22:36.870 --> 00:22:38.130
within Google Cloud.

00:22:38.130 --> 00:22:40.474
Functions is kind of
the main common one,

00:22:40.474 --> 00:22:41.640
but we also have App Engine.

00:22:41.640 --> 00:22:44.070
So App Engine, you're running
an application rather than

00:22:44.070 --> 00:22:45.300
just a single function.

00:22:45.300 --> 00:22:47.400
It has slightly different
scaling behavior.

00:22:47.400 --> 00:22:50.430
So as your App Engine
app is scaling up,

00:22:50.430 --> 00:22:52.420
it'll hit that max
instances limit.

00:22:52.420 --> 00:22:54.420
But then it will also
start routing traffic back

00:22:54.420 --> 00:22:55.795
to existing apps
because they can

00:22:55.795 --> 00:22:57.580
continue serving that traffic.

00:22:57.580 --> 00:22:59.977
So it will still provide
that scaling limit,

00:22:59.977 --> 00:23:01.810
but the behavior will
be slightly different.

00:23:01.810 --> 00:23:03.930
So just something
to take into account

00:23:03.930 --> 00:23:08.530
if you're building an app on
App Engine versus Functions.

00:23:08.530 --> 00:23:11.080
And then to prevent the kind
of economic denial of service

00:23:11.080 --> 00:23:12.264
we can do budget alerts.

00:23:12.264 --> 00:23:13.930
So you can actually
go into your project

00:23:13.930 --> 00:23:18.520
and say, I want to be notified
anytime I hit 50%, 90%, 100%,

00:23:18.520 --> 00:23:21.100
whatever percent you
want of your budget.

00:23:21.100 --> 00:23:23.200
And my favorite
feature of this is

00:23:23.200 --> 00:23:25.870
that you can actually then
trigger Cloud Functions off

00:23:25.870 --> 00:23:27.250
of your budget alert.

00:23:27.250 --> 00:23:29.620
So if you've got a budget
alert saying that your Cloud

00:23:29.620 --> 00:23:31.450
Functions have
exceeded 100%, you

00:23:31.450 --> 00:23:33.610
can trigger more
Cloud Functions.

00:23:33.610 --> 00:23:34.930
Just a fun little thing.

00:23:34.930 --> 00:23:36.372
But that is nice
because then you

00:23:36.372 --> 00:23:38.080
could, say, trigger
a Cloud Function that

00:23:38.080 --> 00:23:40.600
would kill an offending
process or otherwise shut down

00:23:40.600 --> 00:23:42.400
resources or deny access.

00:23:42.400 --> 00:23:46.410
That's a nice little
way of containing that.

00:23:46.410 --> 00:23:50.220
MYLES BORINS: So a great way
to cause problems for yourself

00:23:50.220 --> 00:23:51.930
is leaking credentials.

00:23:51.930 --> 00:23:54.084
If any of you were
paying attention earlier,

00:23:54.084 --> 00:23:55.500
you may have noticed
that I leaked

00:23:55.500 --> 00:23:59.605
all of our database credentials
on screen to all of you.

00:23:59.605 --> 00:24:01.980
Now, we're going to be shutting
that database down later.

00:24:01.980 --> 00:24:03.521
But if you had been
paying attention,

00:24:03.521 --> 00:24:06.840
you probably could have made
some problems for my demo.

00:24:06.840 --> 00:24:09.030
But there are common
credentials and common ways

00:24:09.030 --> 00:24:10.470
that you can leak it.

00:24:10.470 --> 00:24:12.227
Database user and
password, as we saw,

00:24:12.227 --> 00:24:13.560
is a really easy way to leak it.

00:24:13.560 --> 00:24:15.952
Leaking it by writing
it right into your code.

00:24:15.952 --> 00:24:17.910
That's a great way to
say that you accidentally

00:24:17.910 --> 00:24:20.120
put it into your get repo,
you push it to GitHub.

00:24:20.120 --> 00:24:22.800
There's bots that are constantly
doing static analysis of GitHub

00:24:22.800 --> 00:24:24.480
to find these kinds
of credentials.

00:24:24.480 --> 00:24:26.910
You can potentially
leak OAuth credentials.

00:24:26.910 --> 00:24:29.700
This could happen by
potentially sending credentials

00:24:29.700 --> 00:24:32.670
in a request not over HTTPS.

00:24:32.670 --> 00:24:35.490
Anything that's in any of
those hops, if your credentials

00:24:35.490 --> 00:24:38.610
are in plain text there, they
know not only your credentials,

00:24:38.610 --> 00:24:40.770
but where you were
sending them to.

00:24:40.770 --> 00:24:44.820
You can have credential leaks
through improper use of crypto.

00:24:44.820 --> 00:24:46.522
And you can also,
as I was mentioning,

00:24:46.522 --> 00:24:48.480
publish your credentials
accidentally somewhere

00:24:48.480 --> 00:24:50.880
like GitHub or NPM.

00:24:50.880 --> 00:24:55.200
Now, here's an example of
putting the credentials right

00:24:55.200 --> 00:24:56.064
into your code.

00:24:56.064 --> 00:24:58.230
One of the things that
you'll notice about this that

00:24:58.230 --> 00:25:02.640
is particularly problematic
is this mysql.createPool call,

00:25:02.640 --> 00:25:04.860
this is a pretty generic
way of making this call.

00:25:04.860 --> 00:25:07.770
This is very
statically analyzable.

00:25:07.770 --> 00:25:09.990
Bots can crawl, text--
we have BigQuery.

00:25:09.990 --> 00:25:11.730
BigQuery lets you do this.

00:25:11.730 --> 00:25:14.720
You can write parsers that
will go through and just find

00:25:14.720 --> 00:25:17.832
this and leak the
credentials for you.

00:25:17.832 --> 00:25:20.040
So one way that we've been
thinking about fixing this

00:25:20.040 --> 00:25:22.286
and worked on fixing this
is a great new feature

00:25:22.286 --> 00:25:23.910
called environment
variables, something

00:25:23.910 --> 00:25:25.125
I'm really excited about.

00:25:25.125 --> 00:25:27.420
You can now in the deployment
set environment variables

00:25:27.420 --> 00:25:28.461
for your cloud functions.

00:25:28.461 --> 00:25:31.526
You can also set them in
the UI when you deploy it.

00:25:31.526 --> 00:25:33.150
By setting these
environment variables,

00:25:33.150 --> 00:25:36.120
we then inside of our code
could reference the environment

00:25:36.120 --> 00:25:38.310
variables rather
than referencing

00:25:38.310 --> 00:25:40.050
the items themselves.

00:25:40.050 --> 00:25:41.970
And now there is
absolutely no way

00:25:41.970 --> 00:25:44.490
that we're going to be leaking
these credentials using

00:25:44.490 --> 00:25:45.300
our code.

00:25:45.300 --> 00:25:47.640
Now, this is not
foolproof, and we'll

00:25:47.640 --> 00:25:49.960
dig into that in a minute.

00:25:49.960 --> 00:25:51.391
But this is a huge step forward.

00:25:51.391 --> 00:25:53.640
Now, if you didn't want to
put it into the environment

00:25:53.640 --> 00:25:55.080
variables, there's
other ways that you can

00:25:55.080 --> 00:25:56.520
be storing your credentials.

00:25:56.520 --> 00:25:58.890
We have Cloud KMS, which
is cloud hosted keys.

00:25:58.890 --> 00:26:00.650
You could use the
HashiCorp Vault.

00:26:00.650 --> 00:26:02.400
We'll be showing some
other ways to do it.

00:26:02.400 --> 00:26:05.340
So you don't need to put
it right into the process

00:26:05.340 --> 00:26:09.210
into your environment variables,
but it is a really great step

00:26:09.210 --> 00:26:12.930
that you can take without a
lot of engineering investment

00:26:12.930 --> 00:26:15.790
to make your code more
secure right off the bat.

00:26:15.790 --> 00:26:18.040
MICHAEL MCDONALD: I'm really
glad we didn't see anyone

00:26:18.040 --> 00:26:19.576
get up and run out of the room.

00:26:19.576 --> 00:26:21.710
[LAUGHTER]

00:26:21.710 --> 00:26:24.040
OK, so we talked a
lot about credentials,

00:26:24.040 --> 00:26:26.530
but there are also a number of
things in the authentication

00:26:26.530 --> 00:26:28.150
realm of what you're doing
with those credentials

00:26:28.150 --> 00:26:30.310
or how you're checking
those credentials that

00:26:30.310 --> 00:26:32.080
can be potentially
problematic, especially

00:26:32.080 --> 00:26:33.770
in a Serverless world.

00:26:33.770 --> 00:26:36.950
So when we started, Myles
had a single function--

00:26:36.950 --> 00:26:39.400
the save markdown function--
that went ahead and took

00:26:39.400 --> 00:26:42.490
the markdown, parsed
HTML, wrote the database.

00:26:42.490 --> 00:26:44.410
We decided good
code practices, we

00:26:44.410 --> 00:26:46.030
wanted to split those in two.

00:26:46.030 --> 00:26:48.670
We wanted to have a generic
markdown to HTML function,

00:26:48.670 --> 00:26:52.780
because maybe we want to
send our HTML somewhere else.

00:26:52.780 --> 00:26:54.490
And then we also
wanted to restrict

00:26:54.490 --> 00:26:56.980
all that horrific
write to the database

00:26:56.980 --> 00:26:59.860
passwords, and secrets, and code
into its own little separate

00:26:59.860 --> 00:27:00.360
functions.

00:27:00.360 --> 00:27:03.130
So that everyone in
the end user world

00:27:03.130 --> 00:27:06.430
is not directly having
access to our database.

00:27:06.430 --> 00:27:08.680
That was awesome
except for the fact

00:27:08.680 --> 00:27:11.720
that all of these functions
are public on the internet.

00:27:11.720 --> 00:27:15.370
So someone could simply bypass
all of our restrictions set up

00:27:15.370 --> 00:27:19.660
in our markdown to HTML function
and write malicious HTML

00:27:19.660 --> 00:27:20.980
directly into our database.

00:27:20.980 --> 00:27:23.260
It's called execution
flow manipulation.

00:27:23.260 --> 00:27:27.220
And as we move into a world
of micro services or nano

00:27:27.220 --> 00:27:28.070
services--

00:27:28.070 --> 00:27:31.180
I'm trying to make that catch
on, by the way, nano services--

00:27:31.180 --> 00:27:32.950
as we move into that
world, these attacks

00:27:32.950 --> 00:27:34.750
become much more
common, especially when

00:27:34.750 --> 00:27:35.670
you have events--

00:27:35.670 --> 00:27:38.170
you can imagine an event being
triggered from a Google Cloud

00:27:38.170 --> 00:27:40.140
storage bucket
going to a function,

00:27:40.140 --> 00:27:42.190
running to a database,
triggering another event.

00:27:42.190 --> 00:27:46.520
There are a lot of little attack
vectors between those cracks.

00:27:46.520 --> 00:27:48.520
So one of the new features
that we're announcing

00:27:48.520 --> 00:27:51.100
is security controls
between functions.

00:27:51.100 --> 00:27:54.100
So you can set an IAM
policy on a function that

00:27:54.100 --> 00:27:56.980
lets you control access
from not only developers

00:27:56.980 --> 00:27:59.500
but other functions
or other GCP services.

00:27:59.500 --> 00:28:02.490
You could have GCE VM or an
App Engine app securely writing

00:28:02.490 --> 00:28:05.290
to your function as well as
end users who have signed in

00:28:05.290 --> 00:28:06.714
via Google sign in.

00:28:06.714 --> 00:28:08.380
One of the other
things that we're doing

00:28:08.380 --> 00:28:10.780
is we're starting to make
functions private by default.

00:28:10.780 --> 00:28:13.135
So your HTTP functions
will no longer be

00:28:13.135 --> 00:28:15.010
publicly available on
the internet the second

00:28:15.010 --> 00:28:16.420
you deploy them.

00:28:16.420 --> 00:28:19.400
Developers can still use the
command line to call them,

00:28:19.400 --> 00:28:21.670
but you don't have to
release everything publicly

00:28:21.670 --> 00:28:23.370
immediately.

00:28:23.370 --> 00:28:25.260
So we're going to
add IAM policies

00:28:25.260 --> 00:28:26.980
onto both of our functions.

00:28:26.980 --> 00:28:29.850
The first one we're
going to say all users

00:28:29.850 --> 00:28:31.710
can take this
special invoker role

00:28:31.710 --> 00:28:35.580
to invoke our first function,
our markdown to HTML function.

00:28:35.580 --> 00:28:37.170
And on the second
one, we're actually

00:28:37.170 --> 00:28:38.970
going to say we've
given a service

00:28:38.970 --> 00:28:41.100
account to our second
function and we're

00:28:41.100 --> 00:28:44.180
going to say only our
first service account

00:28:44.180 --> 00:28:47.060
and on our first function can
invoke the second function.

00:28:47.060 --> 00:28:50.880
Otherwise, it's closed
off from the world.

00:28:50.880 --> 00:28:53.600
So we have that kind
of IAM security barrier

00:28:53.600 --> 00:28:55.670
between our functions,
and that would

00:28:55.670 --> 00:28:58.100
prevent our malicious
content from coming

00:28:58.100 --> 00:29:01.400
in to the second function.

00:29:01.400 --> 00:29:02.870
But as I mentioned
earlier, we're

00:29:02.870 --> 00:29:05.180
still making it easier
for developers to use it.

00:29:05.180 --> 00:29:07.610
So if you're logged
into the command line,

00:29:07.610 --> 00:29:09.200
you can deploy your function.

00:29:09.200 --> 00:29:11.540
It's deployed privately,
but you can then just

00:29:11.540 --> 00:29:14.360
say gcloud functions call
and call the function

00:29:14.360 --> 00:29:15.684
as you normally would.

00:29:15.684 --> 00:29:17.600
And that will take your
developer credentials,

00:29:17.600 --> 00:29:21.920
pass them through, and let
you invoke that function.

00:29:21.920 --> 00:29:24.710
So if you want to sign up for
this, it's in alpha right now.

00:29:24.710 --> 00:29:27.850
So you can take a
photo of that, sign up,

00:29:27.850 --> 00:29:31.380
and you can start
using it shortly.

00:29:31.380 --> 00:29:33.320
Give everyone a second
to take a look at that.

00:29:37.136 --> 00:29:38.570
Perfect.

00:29:38.570 --> 00:29:40.570
So there's kind of
a parallel to that

00:29:40.570 --> 00:29:44.800
of not only is there a
potential for malicious content

00:29:44.800 --> 00:29:48.400
to come in, but there's
a really high potential

00:29:48.400 --> 00:29:53.060
for the good content that you
have to leave your application.

00:29:53.060 --> 00:29:55.070
So you can imagine,
for instance,

00:29:55.070 --> 00:29:58.360
instead of a malicious call
coming in to your function,

00:29:58.360 --> 00:30:02.110
you can imagine that someone
has maybe done a remote code

00:30:02.110 --> 00:30:04.750
execution vulnerability
in one of your functions

00:30:04.750 --> 00:30:07.287
and is trying to
exfiltrate data.

00:30:07.287 --> 00:30:09.370
So they're going to take
all that markdown content

00:30:09.370 --> 00:30:12.160
and send it to
their own endpoint.

00:30:12.160 --> 00:30:16.270
So the evil.com example
down there below.

00:30:16.270 --> 00:30:18.130
Or you can imagine
a similar thing

00:30:18.130 --> 00:30:22.160
where someone has dumped the
process environment variables,

00:30:22.160 --> 00:30:22.720
which--

00:30:22.720 --> 00:30:24.970
and this is why we were
saying it's maybe not the best

00:30:24.970 --> 00:30:26.560
idea to store all
of your secrets

00:30:26.560 --> 00:30:27.790
in environment variables.

00:30:27.790 --> 00:30:31.415
If someone could eval
console log process n

00:30:31.415 --> 00:30:35.046
and say store that as
their name in the database,

00:30:35.046 --> 00:30:36.670
they could then use
our second function

00:30:36.670 --> 00:30:39.570
to pull all that HTML
content out of the database

00:30:39.570 --> 00:30:40.577
without you knowing.

00:30:40.577 --> 00:30:42.910
We're going to actually have
one of our partners come up

00:30:42.910 --> 00:30:45.250
and talk about how to mitigate
those threats shortly.

00:30:48.260 --> 00:30:50.760
MYLES BORINS: So joked
about this earlier,

00:30:50.760 --> 00:30:54.680
but query injection
is real and alive.

00:30:54.680 --> 00:30:58.130
So the code that I originally
wrote was pretty susceptible.

00:30:58.130 --> 00:31:00.092
Here's a slightly
simplified version of it.

00:31:00.092 --> 00:31:02.300
What you can see that we're
doing that's not so great

00:31:02.300 --> 00:31:05.060
is we're building the query
manually from a string

00:31:05.060 --> 00:31:08.060
and concatenating information
unsanitized that was

00:31:08.060 --> 00:31:10.700
handed over from the internet.

00:31:10.700 --> 00:31:12.750
And one of the things
that could happen here,

00:31:12.750 --> 00:31:15.131
which would be kind
of bad, is someone

00:31:15.131 --> 00:31:16.880
who knows how we have
it done internally--

00:31:16.880 --> 00:31:20.960
or is guessing-- could hand
us off HTML that internally

00:31:20.960 --> 00:31:23.060
is actually breaking
up the string

00:31:23.060 --> 00:31:28.130
and including an extra SQL
call that is, in turn, deleting

00:31:28.130 --> 00:31:29.610
all of our information.

00:31:29.610 --> 00:31:30.499
That's no good.

00:31:30.499 --> 00:31:31.290
We don't want that.

00:31:31.290 --> 00:31:32.373
MICHAEL MCDONALD: No good.

00:31:32.373 --> 00:31:34.310
MYLES BORINS: So we can
fix this by escaping.

00:31:34.310 --> 00:31:36.143
And so one of the things
that's really great

00:31:36.143 --> 00:31:39.440
is MySQL itself, the
module friend PM,

00:31:39.440 --> 00:31:40.709
comes with an escape function.

00:31:40.709 --> 00:31:42.500
This is not the same
as just calling escape

00:31:42.500 --> 00:31:44.007
on the string in JavaScript.

00:31:44.007 --> 00:31:45.590
It comes with a
escape function that's

00:31:45.590 --> 00:31:47.510
going to properly
escape your data

00:31:47.510 --> 00:31:50.480
to be put up into
MySQL so you can still

00:31:50.480 --> 00:31:52.950
build the string without
worrying about doing it.

00:31:52.950 --> 00:31:56.060
Now, while this is
better, there is best.

00:31:56.060 --> 00:31:59.510
So we can avoid
sanitizing altogether

00:31:59.510 --> 00:32:01.730
and rely on the library
to sanitize it for us.

00:32:01.730 --> 00:32:05.450
You'll see the set question mark
syntax that we're using here.

00:32:05.450 --> 00:32:08.360
This is taken directly
from the MySQL readme

00:32:08.360 --> 00:32:10.659
as the preferred way
of posting information.

00:32:10.659 --> 00:32:12.200
And what you can
see that we're doing

00:32:12.200 --> 00:32:13.950
is we're building an
object in JavaScript.

00:32:13.950 --> 00:32:17.990
We're passing that object
over to the MySQL library,

00:32:17.990 --> 00:32:21.470
and we're allowing MySQL to
know how to actually convert

00:32:21.470 --> 00:32:22.221
everything itself.

00:32:22.221 --> 00:32:24.220
One of the things that's
really nice about this,

00:32:24.220 --> 00:32:26.060
too, is not only is
it going to escape,

00:32:26.060 --> 00:32:27.740
but it's going to
smartly escape.

00:32:27.740 --> 00:32:30.440
So in the example
that I had originally,

00:32:30.440 --> 00:32:33.680
I was taking a time stamp with
the JavaScript data object.

00:32:33.680 --> 00:32:36.200
You can actually just pass
that directly this way

00:32:36.200 --> 00:32:38.810
and it will sanitize and turn
the data object into the string

00:32:38.810 --> 00:32:40.920
format that MySQL's expecting.

00:32:40.920 --> 00:32:44.150
So not only are you removing
potential programming errors

00:32:44.150 --> 00:32:45.620
from a security
standpoint, you're

00:32:45.620 --> 00:32:48.020
removing programming
errors from just straight

00:32:48.020 --> 00:32:51.600
up consistency and correct
way of programming.

00:32:51.600 --> 00:32:54.830
So while the other way
of doing it yourself

00:32:54.830 --> 00:32:57.740
works like anything in security,
if there's a way for you

00:32:57.740 --> 00:32:59.780
to not do it, don't do it.

00:32:59.780 --> 00:33:01.550
Let other people who
have done it right

00:33:01.550 --> 00:33:04.880
and made the mistakes
before you do it instead.

00:33:04.880 --> 00:33:10.300
With that, we're going to bring
up Devon Rifkin from Intrinsic.

00:33:10.300 --> 00:33:11.350
Big round of applause.

00:33:11.350 --> 00:33:14.302
[APPLAUSE]

00:33:16.270 --> 00:33:18.800
So Devon, can you tell us a
little bit about Intrinsic?

00:33:18.800 --> 00:33:19.591
DEVON RIFKIN: Sure.

00:33:19.591 --> 00:33:23.020
So Intrinsic is a runtime
enforcement system

00:33:23.020 --> 00:33:25.360
where basically we want to
make it really easy for you

00:33:25.360 --> 00:33:28.000
to choose exactly which
resources your Google Cloud

00:33:28.000 --> 00:33:29.720
Functions can access.

00:33:29.720 --> 00:33:32.750
And so when we talk about how
to get really good security,

00:33:32.750 --> 00:33:34.710
you want to talk about
least privilege, which

00:33:34.710 --> 00:33:36.460
is the idea that your
function should only

00:33:36.460 --> 00:33:39.377
be able to do exactly what it
needs to do and nothing more.

00:33:39.377 --> 00:33:40.960
And this is how we're
going to protect

00:33:40.960 --> 00:33:44.540
against some of the issues that
Mike was talking about earlier.

00:33:44.540 --> 00:33:46.750
So the way that Intrinsic
works is we actually

00:33:46.750 --> 00:33:49.390
sit between the runtime
and all of your code.

00:33:49.390 --> 00:33:52.490
So you've got both application
code and dependency code.

00:33:52.490 --> 00:33:55.420
But what we let you do is we
let you put policies on that

00:33:55.420 --> 00:34:00.220
so that when you try to go
to evil.com it doesn't work.

00:34:00.220 --> 00:34:03.590
So now we're going to actually
look at some improved code,

00:34:03.590 --> 00:34:06.520
sort of what we've
been showing before.

00:34:06.520 --> 00:34:08.500
So this has some of the fixes.

00:34:08.500 --> 00:34:09.670
This is the split out one.

00:34:09.670 --> 00:34:13.570
So this is the first function,
and this one takes markdown,

00:34:13.570 --> 00:34:15.820
and it turns it
into HTML, and then

00:34:15.820 --> 00:34:17.830
it passes it to the
second function.

00:34:17.830 --> 00:34:20.080
So when we think
about what privileges

00:34:20.080 --> 00:34:22.300
does this function
need to do, it's

00:34:22.300 --> 00:34:24.020
not really very much at all.

00:34:24.020 --> 00:34:26.500
In fact, the only
thing it really does

00:34:26.500 --> 00:34:32.110
is down here it just posts
to this post URL which

00:34:32.110 --> 00:34:33.760
is just the second function.

00:34:33.760 --> 00:34:36.699
So when we think about how we
want to restrict this function,

00:34:36.699 --> 00:34:39.940
we really want it so that the
only thing it can possibly do

00:34:39.940 --> 00:34:42.590
is talk to the second function.

00:34:42.590 --> 00:34:44.750
So let's look at how we
do that with Intrinsic.

00:34:44.750 --> 00:34:47.139
With Intrinsic you don't
change your code at all.

00:34:47.139 --> 00:34:48.620
So you keep it the same.

00:34:48.620 --> 00:34:50.920
And the policies, they
apply to all the code.

00:34:50.920 --> 00:34:53.560
Even malicious code can't
get around the policies.

00:34:53.560 --> 00:34:57.160
So we've got just a
single policy here

00:34:57.160 --> 00:34:59.260
where what we do
is we say, hey, we

00:34:59.260 --> 00:35:02.590
want to allow posting to
this particular URL which

00:35:02.590 --> 00:35:05.660
corresponds to our
second function.

00:35:05.660 --> 00:35:10.420
So let's go ahead and look at
what some malicious code might

00:35:10.420 --> 00:35:11.390
look like.

00:35:11.390 --> 00:35:14.110
So this is a little bit
of a contrived example,

00:35:14.110 --> 00:35:15.640
we're just putting
in something that

00:35:15.640 --> 00:35:18.480
changes the URL into evil.com.

00:35:18.480 --> 00:35:20.230
But any of the
dependencies could do that.

00:35:20.230 --> 00:35:23.710
Or if you had a remote code
execution vulnerability,

00:35:23.710 --> 00:35:26.720
they could easily
exfiltrate the data.

00:35:26.720 --> 00:35:28.870
So let's actually
run this function.

00:35:28.870 --> 00:35:31.120
So we're going to
do a malicious call.

00:35:31.120 --> 00:35:34.579
And this just uses the
gcloud functions call.

00:35:34.579 --> 00:35:36.370
And now what's funny
here is it says error,

00:35:36.370 --> 00:35:38.384
but that's actually good.

00:35:38.384 --> 00:35:40.300
That's a good indication
that we're protected,

00:35:40.300 --> 00:35:41.680
but let's take a
look at the logs

00:35:41.680 --> 00:35:43.330
to make sure that that's true.

00:35:45.860 --> 00:35:49.330
So here are the logs, and let me
make this a little bit bigger.

00:35:49.330 --> 00:35:52.350
So we see that we tried
to post to evil.com

00:35:52.350 --> 00:35:54.430
and Intrinsic blocked this.

00:35:54.430 --> 00:35:56.380
So it said, hey, this
is a policy violation.

00:35:56.380 --> 00:35:58.990
You tried to post to
evil.com, and that's not

00:35:58.990 --> 00:36:00.170
in the white list.

00:36:00.170 --> 00:36:02.170
So all the Intrinsic
policies, because we really

00:36:02.170 --> 00:36:06.009
want to focus on the
principle of least privilege,

00:36:06.009 --> 00:36:07.300
it's just all white list based.

00:36:07.300 --> 00:36:08.820
You don't want to say
things you can't do,

00:36:08.820 --> 00:36:10.660
you only want to say
the things you can do.

00:36:10.660 --> 00:36:13.560
And this fits in super well
with how Serverless works,

00:36:13.560 --> 00:36:16.199
where you have lots of nano--

00:36:16.199 --> 00:36:16.990
what are we saying?

00:36:16.990 --> 00:36:18.835
Nano services, right?

00:36:18.835 --> 00:36:21.430
MICHAEL MCDONALD: Catching on.

00:36:21.430 --> 00:36:24.850
DEVON RIFKIN: So this is
great, and this protects you.

00:36:24.850 --> 00:36:27.370
And you can set up
notifications, alerts,

00:36:27.370 --> 00:36:29.470
and stack driver
to notify your team

00:36:29.470 --> 00:36:33.250
that something bad has happened,
something totally unexpected.

00:36:33.250 --> 00:36:36.620
But in the meantime, your
data is completely safe.

00:36:36.620 --> 00:36:38.750
So that's very good.

00:36:38.750 --> 00:36:41.260
We're going to look at
the next function now.

00:36:41.260 --> 00:36:43.006
So this is the second one.

00:36:43.006 --> 00:36:44.380
And this one's
pretty interesting

00:36:44.380 --> 00:36:46.660
because we have another piece
of sensitive data, which

00:36:46.660 --> 00:36:49.060
is the database
password that Myles

00:36:49.060 --> 00:36:52.160
leaked several times already.

00:36:52.160 --> 00:36:54.520
So we've got these
environment variables set up,

00:36:54.520 --> 00:36:56.080
which is pretty good.

00:36:56.080 --> 00:36:58.000
That's pretty good.

00:36:58.000 --> 00:36:59.530
So let's go ahead and run this.

00:36:59.530 --> 00:37:02.690
And we're going to log
the environment variables.

00:37:02.690 --> 00:37:08.710
So we'll run this right here.

00:37:08.710 --> 00:37:10.740
So it inserted it with an ID 90.

00:37:10.740 --> 00:37:14.560
I got rid of Myles' pass the
database results straight

00:37:14.560 --> 00:37:15.460
through.

00:37:15.460 --> 00:37:17.085
Don't want to do that.

00:37:17.085 --> 00:37:18.460
So let's take a
look at the logs.

00:37:21.020 --> 00:37:24.530
Yeah, so kind of as expected
my password is just there.

00:37:24.530 --> 00:37:27.380
And so while this is a
lot better than storing it

00:37:27.380 --> 00:37:30.140
in your code because that means
it shows up in fewer places,

00:37:30.140 --> 00:37:31.982
it still could be exfiltrated.

00:37:31.982 --> 00:37:33.440
Now, luckily, we're
using Intrinsic

00:37:33.440 --> 00:37:38.270
so you can't just post it
to your own attacker server.

00:37:38.270 --> 00:37:41.981
But Mike kind of
alluded to this before.

00:37:41.981 --> 00:37:43.730
If the attacker's super
clever and they're

00:37:43.730 --> 00:37:47.090
trying to really
target you, they

00:37:47.090 --> 00:37:48.800
might just store
it in the database,

00:37:48.800 --> 00:37:51.424
and then go to your blog and
see the password, and then

00:37:51.424 --> 00:37:52.340
mess with it that way.

00:37:52.340 --> 00:37:54.360
So they might exfiltrate
it another way.

00:37:54.360 --> 00:38:01.640
So we've been working on
ways of preventing even

00:38:01.640 --> 00:38:03.170
leaking it in special ways.

00:38:03.170 --> 00:38:07.490
And what we've realized
is you don't actually

00:38:07.490 --> 00:38:09.530
need to see the real
value in your code.

00:38:09.530 --> 00:38:11.780
All you're doing is you're
passing along the password

00:38:11.780 --> 00:38:13.440
to the MySQL database.

00:38:13.440 --> 00:38:15.530
You're not operating
on the actual value.

00:38:15.530 --> 00:38:17.510
So this is a new type
of policy that we've

00:38:17.510 --> 00:38:19.090
introduced called tokenization.

00:38:19.090 --> 00:38:21.090
And what it does is it
takes your real secrets--

00:38:21.090 --> 00:38:25.656
so your database
passwords, or API keys,

00:38:25.656 --> 00:38:27.530
or other sensitive user
data you can imagine.

00:38:27.530 --> 00:38:29.238
Social security numbers,
you don't really

00:38:29.238 --> 00:38:30.860
need to do math on them.

00:38:30.860 --> 00:38:32.424
It turns them into
dummy tokens when

00:38:32.424 --> 00:38:34.340
it's running in the
untrusted code area, which

00:38:34.340 --> 00:38:36.240
is where all your code runs.

00:38:36.240 --> 00:38:38.240
a that means both your
code and third party code

00:38:38.240 --> 00:38:40.340
never see the actual value.

00:38:40.340 --> 00:38:42.740
Then on the way out,
we check to make sure

00:38:42.740 --> 00:38:45.260
that you're connecting to the
thing that we expect you are.

00:38:45.260 --> 00:38:47.720
And if you are, we'll
translate it to the real value.

00:38:47.720 --> 00:38:51.050
But your code never, ever
sees the real secret,

00:38:51.050 --> 00:38:53.900
and that means it's impossible
for that code to leak it.

00:38:53.900 --> 00:38:58.209
So let's see what that
policy might look like.

00:38:58.209 --> 00:38:59.000
So it's super easy.

00:38:59.000 --> 00:39:01.160
Again, we don't change
any of our code.

00:39:01.160 --> 00:39:03.560
And we've got a single
tokenization policy here

00:39:03.560 --> 00:39:05.750
where we say, hey, we've
got this environment

00:39:05.750 --> 00:39:07.670
variable called db password.

00:39:07.670 --> 00:39:09.820
We want to tokenize it.

00:39:09.820 --> 00:39:14.950
And then we want to detokenize
it only when it goes to MySQL,

00:39:14.950 --> 00:39:17.890
and only when it matches exactly
this user database and socket

00:39:17.890 --> 00:39:18.610
path.

00:39:18.610 --> 00:39:21.340
We don't want somebody to
try to steal it by faking

00:39:21.340 --> 00:39:24.340
making another MySQL server.

00:39:24.340 --> 00:39:26.410
And then again, you
don't change any code.

00:39:26.410 --> 00:39:29.030
We've got a single policy.

00:39:29.030 --> 00:39:31.610
We don't need to
make any HTTP calls.

00:39:31.610 --> 00:39:35.780
So let's go ahead and run this.

00:39:35.780 --> 00:39:37.660
So we run this and it's
looking pretty good.

00:39:37.660 --> 00:39:41.510
We do have ID 91, so we're
inserting more things.

00:39:41.510 --> 00:39:45.060
Let's take a look at the logs.

00:39:45.060 --> 00:39:46.010
And this is great.

00:39:46.010 --> 00:39:48.530
So instead of the
real password, it

00:39:48.530 --> 00:39:50.270
shows tokenized by Intrinsic.

00:39:50.270 --> 00:39:53.315
So if an attacker wants to
steal that fake password,

00:39:53.315 --> 00:39:53.940
let them do it.

00:39:53.940 --> 00:39:56.060
It's fine.

00:39:56.060 --> 00:39:57.980
And most importantly,
it still was

00:39:57.980 --> 00:40:01.250
able to post to the MySQL
database and insert it.

00:40:01.250 --> 00:40:04.250
So it worked transparently
but kept you safe.

00:40:04.250 --> 00:40:06.440
And so we're really excited
about these tokenization

00:40:06.440 --> 00:40:09.410
policies, because they
let you just get rid

00:40:09.410 --> 00:40:11.229
of whole classes of attacks.

00:40:11.229 --> 00:40:12.270
And it's really flexible.

00:40:12.270 --> 00:40:14.990
It's not just environment
variables in MySQL databases.

00:40:14.990 --> 00:40:19.310
The sources might be HTTP
responses, or the file system,

00:40:19.310 --> 00:40:20.670
or database entries.

00:40:20.670 --> 00:40:22.310
And it might go to
database entries

00:40:22.310 --> 00:40:24.185
where you want to store
things that you never

00:40:24.185 --> 00:40:25.040
were able to see.

00:40:25.040 --> 00:40:28.850
And you might detokenize
the particular HTTP requests

00:40:28.850 --> 00:40:33.060
if you're using API keys,
and even responses to users.

00:40:33.060 --> 00:40:34.970
So let's say that you
pull a social security

00:40:34.970 --> 00:40:36.630
number from the database.

00:40:36.630 --> 00:40:39.230
Your app code never needs to see
it if it's just displaying it

00:40:39.230 --> 00:40:40.317
to the user.

00:40:40.317 --> 00:40:41.900
So we're really
excited to have people

00:40:41.900 --> 00:40:43.350
start using these policies.

00:40:43.350 --> 00:40:45.850
We think it's going to
really help protect things.

00:40:45.850 --> 00:40:48.320
And thanks, that's what I got.

00:40:48.320 --> 00:40:49.952
MYLES BORINS: Thank
you so much, Devon.

00:40:49.952 --> 00:40:51.573
That was really great.

00:40:51.573 --> 00:40:53.425
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:56.210 --> 00:40:58.310
So we've kind of gone
through a whole class

00:40:58.310 --> 00:41:03.080
of errors that are mostly
solved through programming

00:41:03.080 --> 00:41:04.460
and engineering.

00:41:04.460 --> 00:41:07.790
But engineering is
not just about code.

00:41:07.790 --> 00:41:10.610
If you are running
a team, if you

00:41:10.610 --> 00:41:13.744
are thinking about the
process of your organization,

00:41:13.744 --> 00:41:15.410
there's a whole class
of vulnerabilities

00:41:15.410 --> 00:41:17.810
that can come in
because developers just

00:41:17.810 --> 00:41:20.960
don't have time to deal
with all of your policies.

00:41:20.960 --> 00:41:22.850
If all of your different
security policies

00:41:22.850 --> 00:41:26.424
that you have in place make
for bad developer experiences,

00:41:26.424 --> 00:41:28.340
your developers may end
up working around that

00:41:28.340 --> 00:41:30.890
and making things
even more vulnerable

00:41:30.890 --> 00:41:32.570
than you started with.

00:41:32.570 --> 00:41:35.480
Some examples of poor
developer experience

00:41:35.480 --> 00:41:38.450
include certain kinds
of shared environments.

00:41:38.450 --> 00:41:41.030
Having dev, staging,
and prod all

00:41:41.030 --> 00:41:43.910
use the same database
means that all

00:41:43.910 --> 00:41:46.562
of your different environments
have the same restrictions when

00:41:46.562 --> 00:41:48.770
writing to the database,
which may mean that you have

00:41:48.770 --> 00:41:50.340
to jump through certain hoops.

00:41:50.340 --> 00:41:51.772
MICHAEL MCDONALD: Or they don't.

00:41:51.772 --> 00:41:54.080
MYLES BORINS: Or don't, yeah.

00:41:54.080 --> 00:41:55.910
And you can end up
with unreviewed code

00:41:55.910 --> 00:41:57.590
into production.

00:41:57.590 --> 00:42:01.070
So one thing that you can
do is enforce isolation

00:42:01.070 --> 00:42:04.490
and use a deployment manager
to replicate environments

00:42:04.490 --> 00:42:07.142
to make sure that
you're not getting

00:42:07.142 --> 00:42:08.600
the wrong things
in the wrong place

00:42:08.600 --> 00:42:10.520
or doing things you don't want.

00:42:10.520 --> 00:42:12.020
One of the biggest
problems that can

00:42:12.020 --> 00:42:15.050
happen for people is a slower
manual toolchain, having

00:42:15.050 --> 00:42:18.350
to manually deploy everything,
having it be really slow

00:42:18.350 --> 00:42:21.110
introduces lots of
errors in edge cases.

00:42:21.110 --> 00:42:23.840
Using things like
continuous integration

00:42:23.840 --> 00:42:26.490
and continuous deployment
can really help fix that.

00:42:26.490 --> 00:42:28.640
There's a really awesome
tool called Spinnaker

00:42:28.640 --> 00:42:30.740
which runs on top
of Kubernetes that

00:42:30.740 --> 00:42:32.239
helps you run all this stuff.

00:42:32.239 --> 00:42:34.280
We also have Cloud Builder
is one of our products

00:42:34.280 --> 00:42:38.000
that you can use for automating
the process of Docker.

00:42:38.000 --> 00:42:39.960
If you go and look at
the node sec roadmap,

00:42:39.960 --> 00:42:42.560
some of the stuff in there
that I particularly enjoyed

00:42:42.560 --> 00:42:44.960
was some of the thoughts on
how to separate environments

00:42:44.960 --> 00:42:47.240
and creating different
levels of policy

00:42:47.240 --> 00:42:48.600
for different environments.

00:42:48.600 --> 00:42:50.750
So for example, your
developers can maybe

00:42:50.750 --> 00:42:54.650
use any module that they want
to use while they're in dev.

00:42:54.650 --> 00:42:57.770
And when they move to
staging, your security team

00:42:57.770 --> 00:43:00.620
can start auditing them and
then actually send it back

00:43:00.620 --> 00:43:03.290
to dev if some of
those don't meet

00:43:03.290 --> 00:43:06.140
the level of quality that's
needed to reach production.

00:43:06.140 --> 00:43:07.820
What this does is
it kind of flips it.

00:43:07.820 --> 00:43:10.236
Instead of creating this really
strict policy saying, hey,

00:43:10.236 --> 00:43:11.660
you can't use
anything right now,

00:43:11.660 --> 00:43:13.910
it allows your devs to run
really fast, get the things

00:43:13.910 --> 00:43:16.520
that they need to do
done to figure out

00:43:16.520 --> 00:43:17.970
how to solve the problem.

00:43:17.970 --> 00:43:21.310
And then, once they figured
out those abstractions, on Node

00:43:21.310 --> 00:43:23.780
there's so many different
modules available in NPM.

00:43:23.780 --> 00:43:25.591
So if we use
marked, for example,

00:43:25.591 --> 00:43:27.590
and that DoS wasn't fixed
yet, and they're like,

00:43:27.590 --> 00:43:29.489
oh no, you need another
markdown parser,

00:43:29.489 --> 00:43:31.030
there's seven other
markdown parsers.

00:43:31.030 --> 00:43:34.010
Our security team can even come
up with three or four examples

00:43:34.010 --> 00:43:35.420
that we can switch to.

00:43:35.420 --> 00:43:37.910
But that doesn't stop the
velocity of your team.

00:43:37.910 --> 00:43:40.610
And so instead of creating
problems, we're creating work.

00:43:40.610 --> 00:43:42.060
It's not, hey,
you can't do this.

00:43:42.060 --> 00:43:44.480
It's like, hey,
here's another ticket.

00:43:44.480 --> 00:43:46.175
By changing that
story a little bit,

00:43:46.175 --> 00:43:48.050
we change the way the
developers are working.

00:43:48.050 --> 00:43:52.430
And instead of breaking
velocity, we improve velocity.

00:43:52.430 --> 00:43:56.600
This moves all the way through
your whole process down to how

00:43:56.600 --> 00:43:57.734
you're handling to a thing.

00:43:57.734 --> 00:43:59.900
You need to make sure that
the process that you have

00:43:59.900 --> 00:44:02.390
in place has buy-in
from your team's

00:44:02.390 --> 00:44:04.580
and is not hurting
velocity, but that doesn't

00:44:04.580 --> 00:44:06.270
mean that you compromise.

